  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none mb-12">
    <h2>1. Profile Geometry: Rabbet vs Tongue &amp; Groove vs Built-In Gap</h2>
    <p>
      Understanding the mechanical profile determines how the boards lock together and manage humidity movement:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Historic True Shiplap:</strong> Features an opposing L-shaped notch (rabbet) cut into the top and bottom edges of each board. When stacked, the top rabbet rests over the bottom rabbet. Historically, these fit snugly together without an intentional shadow line to seal out exterior weather in barns and coastal cottages.</li>
      <li><strong>Modern Nickel-Gap Shiplap:</strong> Engineered specifically for interior accent walls. These boards feature an extended shoulder or tongue that automatically creates a consistent 1/8-inch (the exact thickness of an American five-cent nickel) shadow reveal when boards are pressed firmly together.</li>
      <li><strong>Standard Tongue and Groove (T&amp;G):</strong> Features a centered tongue on one edge and a matching groove on the opposing edge. When pushed fully seated, the seam is virtually seamless (unless v-grooved or beadboard profile).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Fastening Mechanics: Blind Nailing vs Face Nailing</h2>
    <p>
      One of the largest trade debates centers on whether you can hide nail holes using blind nailing:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tongue &amp; Groove (Blind Nailable):</strong> Because the tongue extends outward at the top, a carpenter can drive 1-1/2-inch to 2-inch 18-gauge brad nails or 16-gauge finish nails at a 45-degree angle through the base of the tongue into the wall studs. The groove of the next course slides over the tongue, completely hiding every nail head. This eliminates filling and sanding hundreds of nail holes.</li>
      <li><strong>Wide Shiplap (Requires Face Nailing):</strong> Any shiplap wider than 5-1/4 inches (nominal 1x6 or 1x8) will cup across its width as indoor moisture fluctuates if only nailed along one edge. For true stability, 1x6 and 1x8 shiplap requires dual face nails (two 2-inch 16-gauge nails) driven perpendicularly through the face into every stud, which must then be filled with wood filler or spackle.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Direct Profile Comparison Table</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm text-left border border-slate-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-slate-100 text-slate-800 font-bold border-b border-slate-200">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Feature / Factor</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Nickel-Gap Shiplap</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">Tongue &amp; Groove (T&amp;G)</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-4">True Rabbeted Shiplap</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200 text-slate-700">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Joint Mechanism</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Self-spacing tongue or rabbet</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Interlocking tongue &amp; groove</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Overlapping opposing rabbets</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Shadow Reveal</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-mono text-sky-700 font-bold">1/8-in Consistent Shadow</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Flush or V-Groove bevel</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Variable (Flush to 3/16-in)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Fastening Method</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Face nailing (2 per stud)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Blind nailing into tongue (45&deg;)</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Face nailing through lap</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Prep Effort</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Must pre-paint wall or reveals</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Minimal joint pre-painting</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Must pre-paint tongue laps</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-slate-900">Best Application</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Modern Farmhouse Accent Walls</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Plank Ceilings, Wainscoting</td>
            <td class="py-3 px-4">Historic Cladding, Rustic Paneling</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Material Selection: Primed MDF vs Solid Radiata Pine</h2>
    <p>
      Choosing between MDF (Medium Density Fiberboard) and solid wood is critical:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Primed MDF:</strong> Outstanding dimensional stability. MDF does not bow, warp, cup, or expand significantly along its length with humidity changes. It takes paint smoothly without wood grain telegraphing or knot bleed-through. However, MDF is vulnerable to water saturation and is not recommended for high-humidity shower rooms or exterior porches.</li>
      <li><strong>Solid Pine or Cedar:</strong> Authentic wood grain texture and structural rigidity. Essential for damp environments or unheated cabins. However, solid wood expands across the grain by 1/8 inch to 1/4 inch per board across seasons, making proper acclimation and tongue pre-painting non-negotiable.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- Trade Pro Field Note -->
  <div class="mb-10">
    <FieldNote
      title="The Spacer Coin Trick vs Integrated Flanges"
      trade="Interior Millwork"
      content="If you are milling your own shiplap from 1x6 boards on a table saw or router table, buy a $2 roll of real nickels. Real U.S. nickels are precisely 1.95 mm (0.077 inches) thick, and two nickels taped together equal 0.154 inches—perfect for a relaxed 1/8-inch shadow line. Place three nickels along each 8-foot board while tacking it to ensure the line does not sag or pinch out of level."
    />
  </div>

